1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:57,015
PREVIOUSLY ON

3
00:00:57,098 --> 00:00:59,934
<i>When you save up enough,</i>
<i>will you really go to America?</i>

4
00:01:00,018 --> 00:01:02,312
<i>I'm going to leave Joseon, no matter what.</i>

5
00:01:02,395 --> 00:01:03,605
<i>Who's here?</i>

6
00:01:03,688 --> 00:01:07,317
<i>It's the inspector general</i>
<i>of the Government-General of Joseon.</i>

7
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,443
Nice to meet you.

8
00:01:08,526 --> 00:01:12,072
I'm Yoo Philip, Keijo correspondent
for the American WWP News Agency.

9
00:01:12,781 --> 00:01:14,157
- <i>What's the matter?</i>
- <i>My bag…</i>

10
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:15,992
We'll be conducting a brief inspection.

11
00:01:16,076 --> 00:01:17,577
Form a single line, everyone!

12
00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:22,582
- I'm sorry.
- I'm sorry.

13
00:01:23,166 --> 00:01:24,584
<i>Someone stole your wallet?</i>

14
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:26,795
<i>Was there something important in it?</i>

15
00:01:27,462 --> 00:01:28,296
Not really.

16
00:01:29,756 --> 00:01:31,132
Isn't this a pawn ticket?

17
00:01:31,216 --> 00:01:32,634
"Sin Tae-jin."

18
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:34,010
<i>The bag</i>…

19
00:01:34,094 --> 00:01:35,011
What do we do?

20
00:01:35,095 --> 00:01:38,056
Even if the thief finds what's in there,

21
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:41,976
<i>we can only hope nothing goes wrong</i>
<i>until after next Thursday.</i>

22
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:42,894
A bullet?

23
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:45,271
<i>I deliberately approached and married</i>

24
00:01:45,355 --> 00:01:48,108
<i>an American who knew nothing in Shanghai</i>
<i>and came to Gyeongseong</i>

25
00:01:48,191 --> 00:01:49,567
<i>to assassinate Sato.</i>

26
00:01:49,651 --> 00:01:52,779
Thank you for preparing
such a nice meal tonight.

27
00:01:53,863 --> 00:01:55,323
<i>I'll give the bullets back.</i>

28
00:01:55,406 --> 00:01:56,533
Just give me 60 won.

29
00:01:56,616 --> 00:01:58,201
So it's you. The pickpocket.

30
00:01:58,284 --> 00:01:59,661
That's all I have now.

31
00:01:59,744 --> 00:02:01,871
<i>Wait here quietly,</i>
<i>and I'll get the rest later.</i>

32
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:03,373
I didn't expect to see you here.

33
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:04,499
My wallet.

34
00:02:04,582 --> 00:02:05,708
I'd like it back.

35
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:10,630
<i>Are you expecting a cake</i>
<i>from Itsukoshi Department Store?</i>

36
00:02:12,257 --> 00:02:14,008
Make a sound, and you'll die first.

37
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:18,096
Father, stay inside!

38
00:02:20,515 --> 00:02:22,725
<i>His name is Keum Seok-jung.</i>

39
00:02:22,809 --> 00:02:26,229
<i>I wanted nothing more</i>
<i>than to find him and kill him.</i>

40
00:02:26,312 --> 00:02:27,856
He's my husband.

41
00:02:27,939 --> 00:02:29,524
<i>The protest in this village</i>

42
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:30,817
was all me.

43
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:31,901
Shoot.

44
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:34,237
No!

45
00:02:36,156 --> 00:02:38,700
<i>We had our daughter</i>
<i>after ten years of marriage,</i>

46
00:02:38,783 --> 00:02:40,869
<i>and that's how I lost her.</i>

47
00:02:41,452 --> 00:02:44,539
<i>In that moment,</i>
<i>when I had to call her something…</i>

48
00:02:44,622 --> 00:02:45,623
Keum Seo-hee!

49
00:02:46,207 --> 00:02:47,125
<i>Seo-hee</i>

50
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:49,294
<i>was my daughter's name.</i>

51
00:02:49,377 --> 00:02:53,214
<i>I'll report to the governor-general that</i>
<i>the Gugukdan was behind the attack.</i>

52
00:02:53,298 --> 00:02:55,758
They've been a thorn in our side
for years.

53
00:02:55,842 --> 00:02:57,886
We'll use this chance to wipe them out.

54
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:06,603
There's an opening for an interpreter
trainee at the Government-General.

55
00:03:06,686 --> 00:03:09,105
I'll send you to America.
Work at the Government-General.

56
00:03:09,189 --> 00:03:10,940
You want me to be a spy?

57
00:03:11,024 --> 00:03:13,860
<i>Infiltrate the Government-General</i>
<i>as a spy and work for us.</i>

58
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:16,946
<i>After three months, send me to America.</i>

59
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:21,159
<i>Hideo isn't Sato's real son.</i>
<i>Sato adopted a Joseon boy.</i>

60
00:03:21,242 --> 00:03:23,369
<i>He graduated from</i>
<i>the Imperial Japanese Army Academy</i>

61
00:03:23,453 --> 00:03:25,455
<i>and works for</i>
<i>the Government-General of Joseon.</i>

62
00:03:25,538 --> 00:03:27,207
Keep your guard up around him.

63
00:03:27,290 --> 00:03:28,833
Good morning.

64
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:33,421
I'm Keum Seo-hee,
the new interpreter trainee assigned here.

65
00:03:35,924 --> 00:03:38,927
<i>I don't completely trust her either.</i>
<i>And I shouldn't.</i>

66
00:03:39,010 --> 00:03:41,095
But Ga-kyung will do fine.

67
00:03:42,055 --> 00:03:44,515
<i>- Get the inspector general's schedule.</i>
- There.

68
00:03:44,599 --> 00:03:47,602
<i>Give it to the man</i>
<i>with the lemon in his teacup.</i>

69
00:03:48,478 --> 00:03:49,312
<i>Arigato.</i>

70
00:03:49,395 --> 00:03:51,856
<i>But she'll definitely put us in danger.</i>

71
00:04:04,744 --> 00:04:06,412
That was easy.

72
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:14,128
Freeze.

73
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:17,715
You're a spy, aren't you?

74
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:21,261
Keum Seo-hee.

75
00:04:22,303 --> 00:04:23,304
No, I mean,

76
00:04:23,388 --> 00:04:24,347
Lee Ga-kyung.

77
00:05:02,427 --> 00:05:03,761
Do you know where you are?

78
00:05:05,972 --> 00:05:08,725
This is where <i>Josenjings </i>die
one by one every single day.

79
00:05:09,767 --> 00:05:13,146
It's been over 20 years
since Joseon came under Japanese rule,

80
00:05:13,229 --> 00:05:17,150
yet <i>Josenjings </i>still can't face reality
and keep acting up.

81
00:05:19,485 --> 00:05:20,403
Do you get it now?

82
00:05:21,237 --> 00:05:23,614
You're at Jongno Police Station.

83
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:29,912
Why… Why am I here?

84
00:05:32,415 --> 00:05:33,374
Lee Ga-kyung.

85
00:05:34,292 --> 00:05:36,669
A pickpocket from the shantytown
in Gyodang-ri.

86
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:39,714
- Gyeongseong Station is your usual turf.
- No.

87
00:05:40,631 --> 00:05:42,175
I'm…

88
00:05:42,258 --> 00:05:45,303
Keum Seo-hee, an interpreter trainee
at the Government-General of Joseon.

89
00:05:45,386 --> 00:05:47,972
I don't even know who Lee Ga-kyung is.

90
00:05:48,973 --> 00:05:49,932
Fine.

91
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:51,768
Whatever your name is.

92
00:05:55,563 --> 00:05:58,107
Tell me. Who sent you as a spy?

93
00:06:02,403 --> 00:06:04,072
Tell me, who's behind this?

94
00:06:04,947 --> 00:06:06,032
I don't know.

95
00:06:06,115 --> 00:06:08,368
I really don't!

96
00:06:12,413 --> 00:06:14,874
Still going to pretend you don't know?

97
00:06:14,957 --> 00:06:16,376
You still don't know?

98
00:06:16,459 --> 00:06:17,460
Say it already!

99
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:19,045
I'm not a spy!

100
00:06:20,004 --> 00:06:24,384
I told you, I'm an interpreter trainee
at the Government-General!

101
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:26,761
Enough.

102
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:34,352
So you will not confess?

103
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,860
Fine. Then I'll tell you.

104
00:06:44,904 --> 00:06:45,863
Yoo Philip.

105
00:06:46,489 --> 00:06:47,448
It's him, isn't it?

106
00:06:49,742 --> 00:06:50,618
Or

107
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:52,620
is it his wife?

108
00:06:58,751 --> 00:07:01,587
A low-life pickpocket like you
wouldn't become a spy for independence.

109
00:07:02,964 --> 00:07:03,798
Say it.

110
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:06,509
What were you promised
in exchange for being a spy?

111
00:07:08,344 --> 00:07:09,178
Speak!

112
00:07:10,179 --> 00:07:11,139
Was it money?

113
00:07:11,222 --> 00:07:13,391
Tell me who's behind this.

114
00:07:14,225 --> 00:07:15,810
Not only will I spare your life,

115
00:07:15,893 --> 00:07:18,312
but I'll give you
exactly what they promised you.

116
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:22,817
Aren't you afraid of dying?

117
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:26,904
You think taking this to your grave
will free Joseon?

118
00:07:34,996 --> 00:07:36,456
I'll ask you one last time.

119
00:07:39,375 --> 00:07:41,085
Who's behind this?

120
00:07:50,052 --> 00:07:51,137
The one behind me is…

121
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:53,431
Yes. Out with it!

122
00:07:55,975 --> 00:07:57,101
Joseon.

123
00:07:59,395 --> 00:08:00,229
What?

124
00:08:02,064 --> 00:08:03,566
I'm not a spy.

125
00:08:05,151 --> 00:08:08,863
If anyone stands behind me,
it's the 20 million people of Joseon.

126
00:08:08,946 --> 00:08:11,282
Independence for Korea!

127
00:08:11,365 --> 00:08:12,742
You bitch!

128
00:08:25,213 --> 00:08:26,047
You tell me.

129
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:29,091
Who is behind you?

130
00:08:40,645 --> 00:08:47,109
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

131
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:50,112
EPISODE 4

132
00:09:03,584 --> 00:09:05,753
I knew it had to be you.

133
00:09:07,046 --> 00:09:08,881
What on earth are you doing?

134
00:09:08,965 --> 00:09:11,467
How could you pull something like this
behind my back?

135
00:09:11,551 --> 00:09:12,385
I'm sorry.

136
00:09:14,637 --> 00:09:17,390
I planned the whole thing.
Please forgive me.

137
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:23,646
Didn't you trust me
when you made me a spy?

138
00:09:24,397 --> 00:09:25,982
Did you think I'd start bawling

139
00:09:26,065 --> 00:09:28,317
and confess everything
if you put a gun to my head?

140
00:09:31,279 --> 00:09:33,030
This is just ridiculous.

141
00:09:34,156 --> 00:09:35,366
And listen.

142
00:09:35,449 --> 00:09:38,327
If you're going to put on a show,
at least do it properly.

143
00:09:38,828 --> 00:09:42,540
Did you think that just because
you fooled me once, you'd fool me again?

144
00:09:45,418 --> 00:09:46,460
You know what?

145
00:09:47,003 --> 00:09:49,005
I know a thing or two
about Jongno Police Station.

146
00:09:50,214 --> 00:09:52,091
<i>In my neighborhood,</i>

147
00:09:52,174 --> 00:09:56,178
<i>plenty of people were tortured there</i>
<i>and ended up crippled.</i>

148
00:09:56,846 --> 00:09:59,640
<i>At Jongno Police Station,</i>
<i>if you passed out from the beatings,</i>

149
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:02,810
<i>they'd hang you upside down and pour</i>
<i>pepper water up your nose to wake you up.</i>

150
00:10:02,893 --> 00:10:04,854
<i>They'd pull out</i>
<i>your fingernails and toenails</i>

151
00:10:04,937 --> 00:10:07,940
<i>and sear your body</i>
<i>with red-hot metal chopsticks.</i>

152
00:10:08,024 --> 00:10:11,319
But this place?
To call it Jongno Police Station,

153
00:10:11,402 --> 00:10:13,613
it's way too simple.

154
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:16,824
Besides…

155
00:10:25,082 --> 00:10:28,002
<i>When people lose their tempers,</i>
<i>they grab whatever's handy and hit you.</i>

156
00:10:28,085 --> 00:10:30,004
But you didn't use that club.

157
00:10:30,087 --> 00:10:31,881
I guess you felt bad for me, right?

158
00:10:33,341 --> 00:10:34,550
Well…

159
00:10:35,259 --> 00:10:36,844
And the sound of the streetcar.

160
00:10:40,931 --> 00:10:42,183
<i>In the car,</i>

161
00:10:42,266 --> 00:10:44,977
<i>I heard a streetcar passing nearby.</i>

162
00:10:46,228 --> 00:10:49,815
But from where you grabbed me,
no matter which route you take,

163
00:10:49,899 --> 00:10:53,069
we'd never pass a streetcar
on the way to Jongno Police Station.

164
00:10:55,905 --> 00:10:58,032
And if this really were
Jongno Police Station,

165
00:10:58,115 --> 00:11:00,576
why would you blindfold me?

166
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:01,702
Why bother?

167
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:06,123
I'm being awfully kind, aren't I?

168
00:11:06,624 --> 00:11:09,168
You all tried so hard,
I almost feel bad for you.

169
00:11:09,752 --> 00:11:11,671
I'm giving you tips
so you do better next time.

170
00:11:13,714 --> 00:11:16,425
Well then, I'm out.

171
00:11:16,926 --> 00:11:18,928
You don't trust me.

172
00:11:19,011 --> 00:11:20,721
How am I supposed to trust you

173
00:11:20,805 --> 00:11:23,057
and risk my life and work as a spy?

174
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:25,559
So don't think this is unfair.

175
00:11:26,727 --> 00:11:28,688
I'll take the advance
as payment for the beating.

176
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:36,737
About what I said earlier,
"Independence for Korea."

177
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:38,072
Don't read into it.

178
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:40,533
Like I told you,

179
00:11:40,616 --> 00:11:41,867
Joseon's independence

180
00:11:41,951 --> 00:11:44,161
is none of my concern,
and I don't want to get involved.

181
00:11:46,414 --> 00:11:47,707
I only said that

182
00:11:47,790 --> 00:11:49,792
to get your little play over with.

183
00:11:52,712 --> 00:11:54,255
Get your hand off the door.

184
00:11:56,507 --> 00:11:59,885
This gun is loaded,
and I really will shoot if you open it.

185
00:12:06,934 --> 00:12:08,227
Hands off!

186
00:12:11,397 --> 00:12:12,273
Put the gun down.

187
00:12:15,192 --> 00:12:16,318
Step aside, So-ran.

188
00:12:17,236 --> 00:12:18,821
- So-ran!
- So-ran.

189
00:12:22,283 --> 00:12:23,534
Put the gun down.

190
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:55,274
Seo-hee…

191
00:12:57,443 --> 00:12:58,903
was my daughter's name.

192
00:13:01,322 --> 00:13:03,991
My daughter, the one killed
by the inspector general.

193
00:13:12,208 --> 00:13:13,834
Help us. We…

194
00:13:17,171 --> 00:13:19,089
I need you.

195
00:13:32,144 --> 00:13:34,939
Please don't ever keep anything about her
from me again.

196
00:13:35,856 --> 00:13:38,067
Were you really going to shoot me?

197
00:13:38,984 --> 00:13:41,904
Or was it just part of the act
to win her over?

198
00:13:50,079 --> 00:13:51,956
You brought up Seo-hee to her.

199
00:13:52,039 --> 00:13:53,499
Was it part of your plan too?

200
00:13:57,086 --> 00:13:58,462
Don't worry about her.

201
00:13:59,171 --> 00:14:02,633
She's desperate to go to America.
So she won't betray us easily.

202
00:14:02,716 --> 00:14:04,760
That's why she must never find out

203
00:14:05,845 --> 00:14:07,596
<i>that there was never any way</i>

204
00:14:08,973 --> 00:14:11,058
<i>to get her to America in the first place.</i>

205
00:14:18,858 --> 00:14:20,109
<i>Keum Seo-hee!</i>

206
00:14:22,278 --> 00:14:23,279
Help me…

207
00:14:25,573 --> 00:14:26,949
<i>Seo-hee</i>

208
00:14:27,032 --> 00:14:28,701
was my daughter's name.

209
00:14:31,203 --> 00:14:34,164
My daughter, the one killed
by the inspector general.

210
00:14:47,136 --> 00:14:49,722
Oh dear.

211
00:15:11,035 --> 00:15:13,120
You don't need to keep warming that up.

212
00:15:13,704 --> 00:15:15,289
Winter is almost over anyway.

213
00:15:16,081 --> 00:15:17,917
It's still winter in Gyeongseong.

214
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,919
It's much colder than Tokyo, you know.

215
00:15:23,547 --> 00:15:25,758
What's wrong? Can't you breathe?

216
00:15:26,759 --> 00:15:28,552
No, I'm fine…

217
00:15:28,636 --> 00:15:30,387
Let's get you to a doctor.
I'll call a cab.

218
00:15:30,971 --> 00:15:32,264
No, really, I'm fine.

219
00:15:34,099 --> 00:15:35,768
It's just a passing spell.

220
00:15:39,605 --> 00:15:41,106
All right.

221
00:15:41,190 --> 00:15:45,027
If it happens again,
you have to go straight to the hospital.

222
00:15:48,072 --> 00:15:50,449
Hideo, I'm done writing.

223
00:15:51,116 --> 00:15:52,326
All done?

224
00:15:54,745 --> 00:15:56,455
It looks really good.

225
00:15:56,538 --> 00:15:57,873
- Really?
- Yeah.

226
00:15:57,957 --> 00:16:00,417
Let's write them ten more times each.

227
00:16:00,501 --> 00:16:01,627
Okay.

228
00:16:04,505 --> 00:16:05,339
But…

229
00:16:07,675 --> 00:16:08,676
You know…

230
00:16:16,475 --> 00:16:18,310
Once I learn all the lowercase letters,

231
00:16:18,936 --> 00:16:20,688
teach me the Joseon language, please.

232
00:16:22,690 --> 00:16:23,899
The Joseon language.

233
00:16:25,109 --> 00:16:26,902
Since my mom

234
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:29,405
was a Joseon person…

235
00:16:31,407 --> 00:16:32,241
But

236
00:16:33,117 --> 00:16:34,785
promise you won't tell Father.

237
00:16:38,831 --> 00:16:39,957
Okay.

238
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:42,918
I promise I'll teach you.

239
00:16:43,002 --> 00:16:44,086
Okay.

240
00:16:45,629 --> 00:16:47,506
What will you teach her?

241
00:16:48,382 --> 00:16:49,508
I'll tell you later.

242
00:16:53,846 --> 00:16:54,763
Ma'am.

243
00:16:55,848 --> 00:16:56,724
By any chance…

244
00:16:59,101 --> 00:17:01,020
do you know my mother's Joseon name?

245
00:17:01,103 --> 00:17:02,354
Her Joseon name?

246
00:17:03,564 --> 00:17:05,482
No, I don't. I've never heard.

247
00:17:06,275 --> 00:17:08,152
Why ask all of a sudden?

248
00:17:09,194 --> 00:17:10,029
The truth is…

249
00:17:11,989 --> 00:17:14,283
I've been looking for her
since I returned to Gyeongseong.

250
00:17:37,306 --> 00:17:38,849
I'm home.

251
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:42,728
Did you have a good day?

252
00:18:04,291 --> 00:18:07,252
<i>I stumbled upon something</i>
<i>while packing our things in Tokyo.</i>

253
00:18:07,878 --> 00:18:09,880
I think it might help me find my mother.

254
00:18:11,673 --> 00:18:12,674
But…

255
00:18:14,259 --> 00:18:15,803
please don't let Father know.

256
00:18:17,471 --> 00:18:18,305
I won't.

257
00:18:24,728 --> 00:18:26,980
Look, I did all ten.

258
00:18:29,316 --> 00:18:30,234
What is this?

259
00:18:30,734 --> 00:18:31,777
This is…

260
00:18:32,528 --> 00:18:33,821
On Kim Gu's orders,

261
00:18:33,904 --> 00:18:37,074
Lee Bong-chang attempted to assassinate
His Majesty the Emperor in Tokyo.

262
00:18:37,157 --> 00:18:40,035
That bombing attempt
happened only three months ago.

263
00:18:41,995 --> 00:18:43,205
And not long ago,

264
00:18:43,831 --> 00:18:46,458
the subversive group
captured in Hwanghae Province

265
00:18:46,542 --> 00:18:50,796
was sent by the self-proclaimed government
in Shanghai to kill the governor-general.

266
00:18:52,714 --> 00:18:54,049
You know that, don't you?

267
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:55,968
Yes, sir.

268
00:18:56,051 --> 00:18:58,554
Even the Western press

269
00:18:59,555 --> 00:19:01,140
calls them "terrorists."

270
00:19:01,723 --> 00:19:03,934
The whole world sees them
for what they are,

271
00:19:04,017 --> 00:19:08,105
yet we can't even catch one fugitive
right under our noses.

272
00:19:09,398 --> 00:19:11,775
Does that make sense to you?

273
00:19:13,485 --> 00:19:14,695
I have no excuse, sir.

274
00:19:15,821 --> 00:19:20,033
The fugitive from Yoo Philip's place
is still at large,

275
00:19:20,951 --> 00:19:22,494
but we've raided

276
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:24,997
several independence group bases
and arrested many of them.

277
00:19:25,747 --> 00:19:28,542
We're wringing information
from those already in custody.

278
00:19:28,625 --> 00:19:29,835
However,

279
00:19:30,669 --> 00:19:34,256
they're a tough bunch.
Keeping silent is what they do best.

280
00:19:35,174 --> 00:19:36,175
I'm sorry, sir.

281
00:19:39,219 --> 00:19:40,971
It's not that they're tough.

282
00:19:43,056 --> 00:19:44,600
You're just too soft.

283
00:19:46,768 --> 00:19:48,020
Forgive me, sir!

284
00:19:50,147 --> 00:19:53,609
You've lived in Joseon for 20 years,

285
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,696
but you have yet to learn
what Joseon people are like.

286
00:19:59,406 --> 00:20:00,365
Forgive me, sir.

287
00:20:00,449 --> 00:20:02,576
Have you forgotten
what happened to them

288
00:20:02,659 --> 00:20:04,995
when they cried for independence
back in 1919?

289
00:20:07,748 --> 00:20:09,750
Even after seeing all that bloodshed,

290
00:20:10,959 --> 00:20:14,755
they're still carrying on
with their stupid independence movement.

291
00:20:15,547 --> 00:20:16,924
They just won't give it up.

292
00:20:18,383 --> 00:20:19,676
Why do you think that is?

293
00:20:21,803 --> 00:20:24,223
Because there's something in them
that can't be broken

294
00:20:25,557 --> 00:20:27,809
by swords, guns, and torture.

295
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:31,396
And what is that, sir?

296
00:20:33,398 --> 00:20:34,316
Hope.

297
00:20:35,651 --> 00:20:37,402
The delusional hope

298
00:20:38,278 --> 00:20:40,113
that independence will come someday.

299
00:20:41,531 --> 00:20:44,034
A bullet can kill a man,
and that's the end of his life.

300
00:20:45,202 --> 00:20:47,496
But hope is passed down
to the next generation.

301
00:20:49,915 --> 00:20:53,877
So we have to crush it completely.

302
00:20:54,419 --> 00:20:56,713
Right down to the very roots. All of it.

303
00:20:58,882 --> 00:21:01,093
Despite 20 years of our enlightenment,

304
00:21:01,635 --> 00:21:04,388
they're still uncivilized savages.

305
00:21:05,097 --> 00:21:07,015
By doing so, they'll know their place

306
00:21:07,099 --> 00:21:09,268
and come under our civilized rule.

307
00:21:09,351 --> 00:21:11,895
Indeed, sir.

308
00:21:14,189 --> 00:21:15,315
And yet,

309
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:19,236
those pampered elites back in Tokyo

310
00:21:19,736 --> 00:21:20,988
sit in luxury

311
00:21:21,071 --> 00:21:24,074
debating the enlightenment
and peace of Joseon.

312
00:21:28,203 --> 00:21:30,914
Even though they've never
even set foot on this soil.

313
00:21:34,501 --> 00:21:37,045
I was born and raised in Joseon.

314
00:21:38,130 --> 00:21:42,009
No one knows the Joseon people
better than I do.

315
00:21:44,553 --> 00:21:47,180
The future of Joseon will be shaped

316
00:21:47,806 --> 00:21:50,434
by my hands right here.

317
00:21:52,769 --> 00:21:55,022
Yes, sir! You have my absolute loyalty!

318
00:22:08,493 --> 00:22:10,162
We should head out separately.

319
00:22:23,675 --> 00:22:24,593
I'm off.

320
00:22:35,979 --> 00:22:36,855
Damn it.

321
00:22:37,898 --> 00:22:38,815
Lee Ga-kyung.

322
00:22:43,695 --> 00:22:44,654
Who?

323
00:22:45,697 --> 00:22:46,865
I'm Keum Seo-hee.

324
00:22:48,950 --> 00:22:49,993
I'm warning you.

325
00:22:50,077 --> 00:22:53,497
If you put her in danger,
you won't be safe either.

326
00:22:57,709 --> 00:23:01,254
So your warning was about her,
not the Gugukdan?

327
00:23:08,428 --> 00:23:10,472
Next time, I'm keeping it.

328
00:23:40,001 --> 00:23:42,129
<i>He was a Joseon orphan?</i>

329
00:23:43,547 --> 00:23:45,590
<i>That's why he said that…</i>

330
00:23:45,674 --> 00:23:48,552
I was impressed
by your pickpocketing skills.

331
00:23:48,635 --> 00:23:50,470
I was quite hopeless myself.

332
00:23:54,933 --> 00:23:55,767
Ms. Keum.

333
00:23:56,351 --> 00:23:57,185
Yes?

334
00:23:57,269 --> 00:23:59,312
Your job description
doesn't include cleaning.

335
00:23:59,396 --> 00:24:01,189
You don't give me any work,
so I was bored.

336
00:24:01,273 --> 00:24:03,191
How dare I order someone like you around?

337
00:24:03,775 --> 00:24:06,069
If you insist on cleaning,
that's up to you.

338
00:24:06,153 --> 00:24:08,613
Make sure he knows
I didn't force you to do it

339
00:24:08,697 --> 00:24:09,906
to avoid any misunderstanding.

340
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:11,366
Will do.

341
00:24:14,786 --> 00:24:16,538
When will you give me
the pawn ticket back?

342
00:24:18,457 --> 00:24:20,584
Why bring up something we already settled?

343
00:24:21,835 --> 00:24:24,504
But you don't need the pawn ticket

344
00:24:24,588 --> 00:24:26,339
until you find the pawnshop anyway.

345
00:24:29,050 --> 00:24:31,011
So will you give it back
once I find the pawnshop?

346
00:24:31,094 --> 00:24:32,512
Yes, absolutely.

347
00:24:32,596 --> 00:24:33,763
You promise?

348
00:24:33,847 --> 00:24:35,599
I promise.

349
00:24:35,682 --> 00:24:36,558
You have my word.

350
00:24:41,104 --> 00:24:42,063
Yes, this is Sato.

351
00:24:42,981 --> 00:24:44,941
Oh, the English dictionary?

352
00:24:45,025 --> 00:24:46,526
Yes, I'll bring it right away.

353
00:24:48,445 --> 00:24:49,279
Ms. Keum.

354
00:24:50,113 --> 00:24:52,073
- Yes?
- Take this here.

355
00:24:53,783 --> 00:24:56,286
Don't take the elevator. Use the stairs.

356
00:24:56,369 --> 00:24:57,662
I don't use the elevator myself.

357
00:24:57,746 --> 00:25:02,000
Sure. A mere interpreter trainee shouldn't
use the elevator meant for the higher-ups.

358
00:25:03,418 --> 00:25:06,713
You're the inspector general's son
and even you can't ride it?

359
00:25:07,297 --> 00:25:09,090
I said I don't take it.
I never said I can't.

360
00:25:13,303 --> 00:25:15,680
PUBLICATIONS DIVISION
ISHIKAWA

361
00:25:17,724 --> 00:25:19,267
Excuse me, sir.

362
00:25:19,351 --> 00:25:21,394
You're still here?

363
00:25:21,478 --> 00:25:23,688
Where did you say I should deliver this?

364
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:24,898
It's written right there.

365
00:25:28,777 --> 00:25:29,611
What is it?

366
00:25:30,904 --> 00:25:31,863
It's just that…

367
00:25:33,865 --> 00:25:35,992
I can't read Chinese characters very well.

368
00:25:36,785 --> 00:25:37,619
What?

369
00:25:38,954 --> 00:25:41,373
I never got to go to school, so…

370
00:25:42,165 --> 00:25:45,502
I can speak just fine,
but reading and writing are a bit hard.

371
00:25:46,670 --> 00:25:47,504
Give it to me.

372
00:25:52,217 --> 00:25:54,803
In Joseon, you say Publications Division.
In the national language…

373
00:25:56,471 --> 00:25:57,597
In Japanese,

374
00:25:57,681 --> 00:26:00,809
it's read <i>"henshuuka."</i>

375
00:26:03,395 --> 00:26:05,730
- And his name is--
- Mr. Ishikawa.

376
00:26:06,273 --> 00:26:09,442
Ishikawa is a pretty common surname.

377
00:26:10,485 --> 00:26:12,529
I know the basic characters.

378
00:26:13,738 --> 00:26:15,657
Fine, if you say so.

379
00:26:20,370 --> 00:26:21,621
It's on the fourth floor.

380
00:26:22,414 --> 00:26:23,290
Okay.

381
00:26:25,500 --> 00:26:26,793
Did you hurt your leg?

382
00:26:27,669 --> 00:26:30,380
I won't use the elevator. Don't worry.

383
00:26:36,386 --> 00:26:37,721
I'll be on my way.

384
00:26:37,804 --> 00:26:44,603
PUBLICATIONS DIVISION

385
00:27:19,471 --> 00:27:21,848
What are you doing here?

386
00:27:26,269 --> 00:27:28,730
Making sure I don't use the elevator?

387
00:27:29,481 --> 00:27:30,565
What?

388
00:27:31,941 --> 00:27:32,776
Unbelievable.

389
00:27:48,041 --> 00:27:48,875
Hold onto me.

390
00:27:55,048 --> 00:27:56,549
That's okay.

391
00:27:56,633 --> 00:27:57,592
Are you sure?

392
00:27:59,177 --> 00:28:00,011
Yes.

393
00:28:07,644 --> 00:28:08,812
Help me.

394
00:28:10,355 --> 00:28:12,065
Help me!

395
00:28:25,578 --> 00:28:28,623
Don't you know the magic word "please"?

396
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:30,667
Forget it!

397
00:28:40,343 --> 00:28:43,346
Sir, please let me use the elevator
just this once.

398
00:28:43,430 --> 00:28:46,099
I can ride it if I'm with you, right?

399
00:28:46,182 --> 00:28:49,769
It hurts too much. I can't even walk.

400
00:29:05,243 --> 00:29:07,412
Let's make our way down first.

401
00:29:25,972 --> 00:29:26,973
Where are you going?

402
00:29:27,724 --> 00:29:29,684
The elevator's that way.

403
00:29:30,518 --> 00:29:32,187
You aren't allowed to use the elevator.

404
00:29:32,771 --> 00:29:35,815
Even if I'm with you, I can't?

405
00:29:39,736 --> 00:29:40,820
It's because you are…

406
00:29:42,989 --> 00:29:44,407
a Joseon person.

407
00:29:56,586 --> 00:29:58,254
Let's go down to the first floor.

408
00:29:59,339 --> 00:30:01,090
We're going out for work.

409
00:30:03,301 --> 00:30:04,385
Outside?

410
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:13,812
Why aren't you coming?

411
00:30:13,895 --> 00:30:14,771
What?

412
00:30:16,898 --> 00:30:17,732
I'm coming.

413
00:30:21,528 --> 00:30:23,238
HANSEONG MEDICINE CLINIC

414
00:30:23,863 --> 00:30:25,740
Her right ankle is a little swollen.

415
00:30:26,825 --> 00:30:28,451
Please come this way.

416
00:30:31,913 --> 00:30:32,747
Let's go.

417
00:30:33,456 --> 00:30:34,290
Okay.

418
00:30:48,179 --> 00:30:49,556
Why are you standing there?

419
00:30:50,056 --> 00:30:51,474
Either sit down or lie down.

420
00:30:54,018 --> 00:30:56,062
Are you worried about the bill?

421
00:30:56,646 --> 00:30:59,524
I suppose you've already spent
all the money in my wallet.

422
00:30:59,607 --> 00:31:01,526
No, that's not it.

423
00:31:03,653 --> 00:31:04,737
The thing is

424
00:31:05,488 --> 00:31:08,491
I've never been to a clinic
to get treated before.

425
00:31:11,995 --> 00:31:12,912
My goodness.

426
00:31:14,622 --> 00:31:15,498
All right.

427
00:31:17,834 --> 00:31:19,085
What brings you in today?

428
00:31:30,054 --> 00:31:32,015
Her right ankle is quite swollen.

429
00:31:33,850 --> 00:31:34,851
Right.

430
00:31:35,435 --> 00:31:37,103
I sprained my ankle.

431
00:31:39,606 --> 00:31:40,440
I see.

432
00:31:46,821 --> 00:31:49,032
Aren't you going to ask how I got hurt?

433
00:31:51,034 --> 00:31:52,410
Are you hurt anywhere else?

434
00:31:52,493 --> 00:31:53,578
Yes.

435
00:31:54,412 --> 00:31:56,247
It hurts here too.

436
00:31:56,831 --> 00:31:59,000
I've got bruises everywhere.

437
00:31:59,584 --> 00:32:01,210
<i>You're the ones who beat me up!</i>

438
00:32:08,009 --> 00:32:10,553
I'll let the acupuncture needles sit

439
00:32:11,095 --> 00:32:12,555
for 20 minutes

440
00:32:12,639 --> 00:32:14,849
and come pull them out
after checking on other patients.

441
00:32:14,933 --> 00:32:16,809
Give her an ointment for bruises too.

442
00:32:17,727 --> 00:32:19,479
It's fine. I have ointment at home.

443
00:32:20,313 --> 00:32:21,189
We'll take one.

444
00:32:22,148 --> 00:32:22,982
Just a moment.

445
00:32:33,159 --> 00:32:35,995
Doctor! Please help my son!

446
00:32:36,079 --> 00:32:37,747
Over here, Doctor!

447
00:32:37,830 --> 00:32:38,957
- Doctor!
- Over here!

448
00:32:39,040 --> 00:32:42,251
- Doctor!
- Please take a look at my boy, Doctor!

449
00:32:43,252 --> 00:32:44,545
What happened?

450
00:32:44,629 --> 00:32:45,630
Please take a look.

451
00:32:50,551 --> 00:32:53,888
"16-year-old girl honored
with the governor-general's award."

452
00:32:53,972 --> 00:32:55,807
That's quite a nice drawing.

453
00:32:56,307 --> 00:32:58,559
But what does it have to do with anything?

454
00:32:58,643 --> 00:33:01,479
Well, the governor-general
has always attended

455
00:33:01,562 --> 00:33:04,232
the Joseon Painting Exhibition himself
to present the awards.

456
00:33:04,315 --> 00:33:06,776
But he's in Tokyo right now.

457
00:33:07,610 --> 00:33:09,570
Then who'll present the awards?

458
00:33:12,657 --> 00:33:14,242
The inspector general will.

459
00:33:14,784 --> 00:33:15,994
Exactly.

460
00:33:16,077 --> 00:33:19,664
The awards ceremony is at the Keijo
Imperial University Library auditorium

461
00:33:19,747 --> 00:33:21,207
at 7:00 p.m. the day after tomorrow.

462
00:33:21,290 --> 00:33:24,711
The day after tomorrow is too soon.

463
00:33:24,794 --> 00:33:26,546
No, no.

464
00:33:26,629 --> 00:33:28,172
It's not impossible.

465
00:33:29,215 --> 00:33:31,092
We have to seize the opportunity
when it comes.

466
00:33:31,592 --> 00:33:35,179
We still need to confirm
if Sato will actually be there.

467
00:33:35,263 --> 00:33:37,348
Hold on. Who has the schedule?

468
00:33:37,432 --> 00:33:38,641
- Pass it over.
- Okay.

469
00:33:39,642 --> 00:33:40,560
Look at the date.

470
00:33:43,229 --> 00:33:44,439
There it is, the speech.

471
00:33:45,314 --> 00:33:47,734
Sato will be at the awards ceremony.

472
00:33:47,817 --> 00:33:49,819
This is Sato's schedule she copied,
isn't it?

473
00:33:49,902 --> 00:33:52,030
It says "speech" right here.

474
00:33:52,113 --> 00:33:54,115
When she first brought this to us,

475
00:33:54,198 --> 00:33:55,950
we couldn't exactly trust her.

476
00:33:56,034 --> 00:33:59,454
We had no idea what kind of speech
he was giving or where.

477
00:33:59,537 --> 00:34:01,664
Now the same schedule is in the newspaper.

478
00:34:01,748 --> 00:34:03,666
What could be more certain than this?

479
00:34:05,877 --> 00:34:09,380
This is the first piece of intel
from the girl you vouched for, So-ran.

480
00:34:09,464 --> 00:34:10,882
We're doing this, got it?

481
00:34:10,965 --> 00:34:12,550
- We're doing this, okay?
- Okay.

482
00:34:12,633 --> 00:34:13,801
Well done.

483
00:34:13,885 --> 00:34:17,013
Listen, don't say anything to her.

484
00:34:17,096 --> 00:34:20,349
If she tips them off,
we're in serious trouble.

485
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:25,229
Sato.

486
00:34:25,313 --> 00:34:27,398
You'll be a dead man that day.

487
00:34:29,192 --> 00:34:30,276
Let's do this!

488
00:34:30,359 --> 00:34:31,194
Let's do it.

489
00:34:40,828 --> 00:34:42,246
What's taking him so long?

490
00:34:43,539 --> 00:34:45,374
We're nowhere near 20 minutes.

491
00:34:50,922 --> 00:34:52,423
I'm all better now.

492
00:34:52,507 --> 00:34:53,341
Ms. Keum.

493
00:34:54,467 --> 00:34:55,635
No.

494
00:34:55,718 --> 00:34:59,055
Why even bother coming here
if you won't listen to the doctor?

495
00:34:59,138 --> 00:35:00,890
I never said I wanted to come.

496
00:35:03,935 --> 00:35:05,019
You can't just leave.

497
00:35:05,645 --> 00:35:08,773
We need to keep your ankle still.
Sit down. I'll bandage it.

498
00:35:13,653 --> 00:35:15,321
Interpreter trainee Keum Seo-hee.

499
00:35:15,404 --> 00:35:18,074
Don't you know
what "strict hierarchical obedience"…

500
00:35:18,157 --> 00:35:19,826
Right, you don't know Chinese characters.

501
00:35:19,909 --> 00:35:22,161
No, I know that much.

502
00:35:22,245 --> 00:35:24,580
Then write it out in Chinese characters.

503
00:35:24,664 --> 00:35:25,957
If you can, you're free to go.

504
00:35:27,375 --> 00:35:28,292
Give me your palm.

505
00:35:29,502 --> 00:35:30,336
What?

506
00:35:30,419 --> 00:35:33,172
Give me your hand.
You told me to write the characters.

507
00:35:36,050 --> 00:35:36,884
My hand…

508
00:35:39,262 --> 00:35:42,390
<i>Sang</i>, <i>myeong</i>, <i>ha</i>…

509
00:35:44,892 --> 00:35:45,726
<i>bok</i>.

510
00:35:46,519 --> 00:35:47,937
There, "<i>Sangmyeonghabok</i>."

511
00:35:49,355 --> 00:35:50,565
That "<i>bok</i>" is for the abdomen.

512
00:35:51,357 --> 00:35:52,191
What?

513
00:35:52,275 --> 00:35:55,278
The "<i>bok</i>" in the phrase means "to obey."
But what you wrote

514
00:35:55,361 --> 00:35:56,195
means the "abdomen."

515
00:35:56,279 --> 00:35:58,030
I know.

516
00:35:58,114 --> 00:35:59,740
I got it mixed up for a moment.

517
00:36:00,449 --> 00:36:02,285
I'll write again. Give me your hand.

518
00:36:04,579 --> 00:36:05,955
There. Use that.

519
00:36:16,132 --> 00:36:16,966
<i>Sang</i>.

520
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:19,051
<i>Myeong</i>.

521
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:20,678
<i>Ha</i>.

522
00:36:57,381 --> 00:36:59,342
Do you know how to wrap a bandage?

523
00:37:00,384 --> 00:37:01,594
I learned it at school.

524
00:37:02,386 --> 00:37:03,471
At school?

525
00:37:05,014 --> 00:37:07,225
Do they teach you
how to do this at school?

526
00:37:11,520 --> 00:37:13,564
I wouldn't know, I never went to school.

527
00:37:14,941 --> 00:37:16,567
When you enter the Army Academy,

528
00:37:16,651 --> 00:37:18,569
the first thing they teach you
is first aid.

529
00:37:19,946 --> 00:37:20,821
Right.

530
00:37:21,405 --> 00:37:24,116
You graduated from
the Imperial Japanese Army Academy.

531
00:37:36,587 --> 00:37:37,505
By the way,

532
00:37:38,089 --> 00:37:41,467
why did you become an interpreter
instead of a soldier?

533
00:37:43,177 --> 00:37:44,011
All done.

534
00:37:45,263 --> 00:37:46,889
Get up and try walking.

535
00:37:49,642 --> 00:37:50,851
Okay.

536
00:37:59,026 --> 00:38:00,111
Is it feeling better?

537
00:38:01,487 --> 00:38:02,446
Yes.

538
00:38:09,245 --> 00:38:11,622
This is when you're supposed to say
"thank you."

539
00:38:13,291 --> 00:38:14,792
Fine. Thank you.

540
00:38:16,335 --> 00:38:17,962
I'm not lying. I mean it.

541
00:38:20,798 --> 00:38:21,632
All right.

542
00:38:29,307 --> 00:38:30,808
About what you said yesterday.

543
00:38:31,517 --> 00:38:32,351
What?

544
00:38:33,853 --> 00:38:34,895
"I like you."

545
00:38:37,606 --> 00:38:39,108
You said yesterday

546
00:38:39,859 --> 00:38:41,027
that you liked me.

547
00:38:43,279 --> 00:38:45,323
You claimed you weren't lying.

548
00:38:49,952 --> 00:38:51,454
Don't let Mr. Yoo know.

549
00:38:54,540 --> 00:38:57,418
I need to keep things amicable with him.

550
00:38:59,462 --> 00:39:01,172
I hope you won't play dumb.

551
00:39:02,131 --> 00:39:04,425
You used that
to join the Government-General.

552
00:39:06,135 --> 00:39:08,971
You said I was lying.

553
00:39:10,222 --> 00:39:12,266
You said you meant what you said.

554
00:39:16,312 --> 00:39:18,147
Of course, you were probably lying.

555
00:39:18,773 --> 00:39:21,025
But if, by any chance,
you really meant it…

556
00:39:24,362 --> 00:39:26,072
don't let Mr. Yoo find out.

557
00:39:30,701 --> 00:39:31,744
Here you go.

558
00:39:31,827 --> 00:39:33,162
Thank you.

559
00:39:33,245 --> 00:39:36,540
DISPENSARY

560
00:39:40,461 --> 00:39:41,462
Here you go.

561
00:39:42,254 --> 00:39:44,256
Please wait. I'll go grab some ointment.

562
00:39:52,264 --> 00:39:54,683
Hold on to me. I don't mind.

563
00:39:55,893 --> 00:39:57,061
There's nowhere to sit.

564
00:40:00,439 --> 00:40:01,524
Thank you.

565
00:40:07,988 --> 00:40:09,031
Does he hit you?

566
00:40:10,241 --> 00:40:11,075
Sorry?

567
00:40:11,951 --> 00:40:14,662
Your wrists, the bruises, and ankles.

568
00:40:16,205 --> 00:40:17,623
Did he do that to you?

569
00:40:19,333 --> 00:40:20,543
Who are you talking…

570
00:40:24,130 --> 00:40:26,006
- No, it's not like that.
- Seo-hee.

571
00:40:32,638 --> 00:40:34,014
It's you, Mr. Hideo.

572
00:40:35,808 --> 00:40:37,518
I wasn't sure it was you from behind.

573
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:46,193
I didn't expect to see you here.

574
00:40:46,277 --> 00:40:47,236
Indeed.

575
00:40:49,530 --> 00:40:51,657
I'm here to get some medicine for my wife.

576
00:40:51,740 --> 00:40:54,285
I hear this is an excellent clinic.

577
00:40:54,910 --> 00:40:56,871
Is your wife still unwell?

578
00:40:56,954 --> 00:40:57,830
Yes.

579
00:40:58,581 --> 00:41:01,000
It seems it'll take some time
for her to recover.

580
00:41:11,635 --> 00:41:15,139
Can Seo-hee go with me from here?

581
00:41:20,436 --> 00:41:21,979
Just today, if you don't mind.

582
00:41:25,608 --> 00:41:27,610
Yes. I'd appreciate it.

583
00:41:48,964 --> 00:41:51,884
It was nice seeing you today.
Have a good day.

584
00:41:51,967 --> 00:41:52,843
Bye.

585
00:41:55,679 --> 00:41:57,473
Get your arm off me.

586
00:41:57,556 --> 00:42:00,267
We're lovers. What's the problem, honey?

587
00:42:04,772 --> 00:42:06,023
Son of a bitch.

588
00:42:06,607 --> 00:42:08,234
Still, this is a bit much.

589
00:42:08,317 --> 00:42:10,361
Sato Hideo is watching us.

590
00:42:14,573 --> 00:42:16,450
Why would she date a man like that?

591
00:42:30,923 --> 00:42:33,842
Did you think bringing him there
would scare us?

592
00:42:33,926 --> 00:42:35,886
Why not just go ahead and tip them off?

593
00:42:35,970 --> 00:42:37,179
Look.

594
00:42:37,263 --> 00:42:39,640
I had no idea that was your den.

595
00:42:39,723 --> 00:42:41,100
I didn't bring him there.

596
00:42:41,183 --> 00:42:42,184
He's the one who…

597
00:42:44,645 --> 00:42:45,771
Just stay still.

598
00:42:58,075 --> 00:42:58,909
Mr. Yoo Philip.

599
00:43:07,334 --> 00:43:08,460
Mr. Hideo.

600
00:43:10,170 --> 00:43:11,797
I didn't think you'd catch us like this.

601
00:43:12,881 --> 00:43:14,091
What are you doing here?

602
00:43:14,675 --> 00:43:16,302
I thought you'd already left.

603
00:43:21,849 --> 00:43:23,017
The ointment.

604
00:43:23,100 --> 00:43:24,685
You should take it.

605
00:43:27,730 --> 00:43:28,564
Right.

606
00:43:29,189 --> 00:43:30,149
Thank you.

607
00:43:39,283 --> 00:43:40,534
So what you're saying is,

608
00:43:40,618 --> 00:43:43,621
you couldn't get around the building
because your ankle hurt.

609
00:43:43,704 --> 00:43:45,497
That's why you found out nothing?

610
00:43:45,581 --> 00:43:46,457
Yes.

611
00:43:47,082 --> 00:43:49,627
On top of that,
someone made me leave work early.

612
00:43:50,377 --> 00:43:53,380
How convenient. Now you have an excuse
for your incompetence.

613
00:43:53,464 --> 00:43:54,632
What did you just say?

614
00:43:55,299 --> 00:43:57,551
Whose fault is it that I hurt my ankle?

615
00:43:58,927 --> 00:44:00,012
All right.

616
00:44:00,095 --> 00:44:03,349
I understand. It must've been hard
getting around with your ankle.

617
00:44:04,141 --> 00:44:07,186
Watch how things go
until you're better for the time being.

618
00:44:09,021 --> 00:44:10,397
Okay, I will.

619
00:44:15,235 --> 00:44:16,236
Good night.

620
00:44:18,614 --> 00:44:20,157
Read this.

621
00:44:21,742 --> 00:44:22,826
What is it?

622
00:44:23,494 --> 00:44:25,621
It's intel on the staff
at the Government-General.

623
00:44:25,704 --> 00:44:27,748
The Satos aren't on it,
but it should help.

624
00:44:30,459 --> 00:44:31,293
Okay.

625
00:44:33,629 --> 00:44:35,089
Who put this together?

626
00:44:39,301 --> 00:44:42,513
Have you ever had a spy
inside the Government-General before?

627
00:44:42,596 --> 00:44:44,181
No, we haven't.

628
00:44:44,264 --> 00:44:46,558
We've been compiling this intel
for a long time.

629
00:44:48,394 --> 00:44:49,228
Okay.

630
00:44:51,105 --> 00:44:52,815
- As for Sato Hideo--
- What?

631
00:44:57,403 --> 00:44:58,612
What about him?

632
00:45:00,072 --> 00:45:02,241
Did anything else happen at the clinic?

633
00:45:04,868 --> 00:45:06,078
"I like you."

634
00:45:08,914 --> 00:45:09,832
No.

635
00:45:10,833 --> 00:45:12,084
Nothing happened.

636
00:45:12,167 --> 00:45:13,168
Well…

637
00:45:13,711 --> 00:45:15,546
He took me to the clinic

638
00:45:15,629 --> 00:45:17,256
because he thinks I'm someone's lover.

639
00:45:17,339 --> 00:45:19,550
He was probably told to be nice to me.

640
00:45:23,095 --> 00:45:23,929
Right.

641
00:45:25,013 --> 00:45:25,848
Well then.

642
00:45:28,183 --> 00:45:29,143
Wait, Seo-hee.

643
00:45:37,359 --> 00:45:39,236
I'm sorry you got hurt.

644
00:45:45,576 --> 00:45:46,827
It's all right.

645
00:45:47,828 --> 00:45:50,831
I've been beaten worse. This is nothing.

646
00:45:54,001 --> 00:45:55,002
By the way,

647
00:45:55,627 --> 00:45:57,588
you two aren't really married, are you?

648
00:45:59,840 --> 00:46:01,550
But he likes you, doesn't he?

649
00:46:04,303 --> 00:46:06,972
I guess you've read
popular fiction too much.

650
00:46:08,348 --> 00:46:09,933
I could tell from his eyes.

651
00:46:10,017 --> 00:46:11,935
He can't take his eyes off you.

652
00:46:12,019 --> 00:46:13,896
Married men aren't usually like that.

653
00:46:16,231 --> 00:46:17,316
But then…

654
00:46:18,400 --> 00:46:21,403
What about Seo-hee's father…

655
00:46:28,827 --> 00:46:31,371
I'm sorry. I shouldn't have brought it up.

656
00:46:35,042 --> 00:46:37,252
You don't like calling me Seo-hee,

657
00:46:38,212 --> 00:46:39,463
do you?

658
00:46:45,636 --> 00:46:49,139
Well, there's nothing we can do about it.

659
00:46:53,852 --> 00:46:54,728
All done.

660
00:46:55,229 --> 00:46:56,063
Get some sleep.

661
00:47:00,609 --> 00:47:02,152
Oh, I forgot one thing.

662
00:47:02,736 --> 00:47:04,613
According to the schedule you copied,

663
00:47:04,696 --> 00:47:08,325
Sato is supposed to give a speech
at 7:00 p.m. Monday.

664
00:47:08,408 --> 00:47:10,828
Did you hear anything else
about that schedule?

665
00:47:10,911 --> 00:47:11,954
A speech?

666
00:47:13,247 --> 00:47:14,248
There was that, huh?

667
00:47:14,331 --> 00:47:17,376
I was scrambling to copy the schedule.

668
00:47:17,960 --> 00:47:20,671
I didn't hear anything else
about the speech.

669
00:47:22,548 --> 00:47:23,382
I see.

670
00:47:25,843 --> 00:47:27,052
Wait…

671
00:47:27,135 --> 00:47:28,804
The day after tomorrow is Monday, right?

672
00:47:29,888 --> 00:47:31,348
At 7:00 p.m…

673
00:47:31,431 --> 00:47:33,183
Making this book is so exhausting.

674
00:47:33,267 --> 00:47:37,145
Our chief has no pull, so other divisions
get all the good time slots.

675
00:47:37,229 --> 00:47:39,940
Of all the times he could've picked,
he chose that one?

676
00:47:40,023 --> 00:47:43,110
If we report to the inspector general
at 7:00 p.m. the day after tomorrow,

677
00:47:43,193 --> 00:47:44,736
when are we supposed to go home?

678
00:47:46,029 --> 00:47:47,698
Enough dawdling!

679
00:47:47,781 --> 00:47:49,366
Get in here.

680
00:47:49,449 --> 00:47:50,784
Yes, coming!

681
00:47:51,827 --> 00:47:55,581
The Publications Division is working
on a book about the history of Joseon.

682
00:47:55,664 --> 00:47:58,959
The briefing for the inspector general
is at 7:00 p.m. the day after tomorrow.

683
00:47:59,543 --> 00:48:02,379
The Publications Division chief
has no influence,

684
00:48:02,462 --> 00:48:04,840
so the other divisions
took all the good slots

685
00:48:04,923 --> 00:48:06,758
and they're stuck with a late slot.

686
00:48:06,842 --> 00:48:08,760
The staff were grumbling about it.

687
00:48:09,428 --> 00:48:12,723
They said they'd never get home
if the report started at 7:00 p.m.

688
00:48:14,600 --> 00:48:15,434
Are you sure?

689
00:48:16,643 --> 00:48:19,313
Yes. I overheard it
when I was out on an errand.

690
00:48:19,396 --> 00:48:22,357
That's 7:00 p.m. on Monday.

691
00:48:22,441 --> 00:48:23,942
I suppose the inspector general

692
00:48:24,026 --> 00:48:26,695
has decided to spend the whole week
getting briefings.

693
00:48:27,613 --> 00:48:28,655
Well, why do you ask?

694
00:48:30,282 --> 00:48:31,199
No, it's nothing.

695
00:48:31,783 --> 00:48:33,911
You look like you're doubting me.

696
00:48:34,786 --> 00:48:36,288
I earn my keep.

697
00:48:38,040 --> 00:48:39,917
I know. I trust you.

698
00:49:19,331 --> 00:49:20,207
No, wait.

699
00:49:20,749 --> 00:49:22,084
What do you mean?

700
00:49:23,877 --> 00:49:26,254
So, So-ran, you're saying

701
00:49:26,838 --> 00:49:30,592
Sato will be at the Government-General
at the time of the ceremony tomorrow?

702
00:49:32,260 --> 00:49:33,804
- Did she tell you that?
- Yes.

703
00:49:34,346 --> 00:49:35,263
At the same time,

704
00:49:35,347 --> 00:49:38,558
the Publications Division will give
a briefing on the Joseon history project.

705
00:49:40,310 --> 00:49:42,813
The girl who won first place
in the art competition

706
00:49:42,896 --> 00:49:45,649
said the inspector general
was supposed to present the award.

707
00:49:46,483 --> 00:49:49,027
I confirmed it through the girl's mother.

708
00:49:49,111 --> 00:49:52,155
I looked into it,
and she works at Joil Textile Factory.

709
00:49:52,239 --> 00:49:55,492
The school asked her parents to attend
as the inspector general would come.

710
00:49:55,575 --> 00:49:58,161
I confirmed they'd requested
a shift change at work.

711
00:49:58,245 --> 00:50:01,665
The Jongno Police Station was instructed
to assign officers tomorrow

712
00:50:01,748 --> 00:50:04,918
because Sato will be attending
the awards ceremony.

713
00:50:05,460 --> 00:50:07,796
I overheard the police talking
while I was camping out

714
00:50:07,879 --> 00:50:09,715
at the udon shop near the police station.

715
00:50:10,382 --> 00:50:13,510
The Keijo Imperial University library,
where the ceremony is being held,

716
00:50:13,593 --> 00:50:16,221
was told to prepare the VIP room
in the auditorium.

717
00:50:16,304 --> 00:50:18,223
I confirmed it with a library cleaner.

718
00:50:18,306 --> 00:50:20,642
She's the one who copied the schedule.

719
00:50:20,726 --> 00:50:24,438
She said she overheard it,
but she could have heard wrong.

720
00:50:24,521 --> 00:50:25,480
That's right.

721
00:50:26,606 --> 00:50:27,607
So-ran.

722
00:50:28,442 --> 00:50:31,361
We know she's quick
with her hands and sharp,

723
00:50:32,112 --> 00:50:34,614
but in this situation,
shouldn't you trust us?

724
00:50:53,383 --> 00:50:56,344
16-YEAR-OLD GIRL
HONORED WITH THE GOVERNOR-GENERAL'S AWARD

725
00:51:01,933 --> 00:51:05,270
THE DAY OF THE OPERATION

726
00:51:32,506 --> 00:51:34,174
Do you have something to tell me?

727
00:51:35,425 --> 00:51:36,343
Well…

728
00:51:37,135 --> 00:51:39,471
You look quite busy today.

729
00:51:39,554 --> 00:51:42,099
You haven't left your desk
since this morning.

730
00:51:42,182 --> 00:51:45,685
Are you preparing for the report
to the inspector general?

731
00:51:45,769 --> 00:51:48,355
The chief reports
for the Foreign Affairs Division.

732
00:51:48,438 --> 00:51:49,648
I'm just an interpreter.

733
00:51:52,067 --> 00:51:53,276
I see.

734
00:51:55,278 --> 00:51:58,281
How do you know about the briefing
for the inspector general?

735
00:52:01,034 --> 00:52:02,494
I don't think I mentioned it.

736
00:52:04,037 --> 00:52:06,540
I overheard some people
talking in the hallway.

737
00:52:11,336 --> 00:52:12,212
Ms. Keum.

738
00:52:13,130 --> 00:52:13,964
Yes?

739
00:52:14,631 --> 00:52:17,801
Try not to wander
around the Government-General.

740
00:52:17,884 --> 00:52:19,136
People are watching.

741
00:52:19,678 --> 00:52:21,388
There aren't many female employees here.

742
00:52:22,097 --> 00:52:23,598
Okay, I understand.

743
00:52:26,101 --> 00:52:29,521
It's a beautiful day, isn't it?

744
00:52:52,169 --> 00:52:53,003
Next.

745
00:53:21,448 --> 00:53:22,741
WWP NEWS

746
00:54:02,697 --> 00:54:03,907
That's a wrap.

747
00:54:14,668 --> 00:54:17,545
DONGSUNG TEXTILES

748
00:54:24,761 --> 00:54:26,471
<i>There's a second floor</i>
<i>over the drapery shop.</i>

749
00:54:27,138 --> 00:54:29,849
<i>So-ran, wait there in ambush.</i>

750
00:54:29,933 --> 00:54:34,271
<i>If Sato goes from the Government-General</i>
<i>to Keijo Imperial University,</i>

751
00:54:34,354 --> 00:54:37,816
he has to go by Angukjeong
and pass in front of Changdeokgung Palace.

752
00:54:37,899 --> 00:54:39,192
This is Ihwajeong.

753
00:54:39,276 --> 00:54:41,111
Farther down,
it's a three-way intersection.

754
00:54:41,194 --> 00:54:42,445
There's a straight road here.

755
00:54:42,529 --> 00:54:44,572
If you take this one, it's a detour,

756
00:54:44,656 --> 00:54:46,533
but you can
get to Keijo Imperial University.

757
00:54:46,616 --> 00:54:49,995
So we'll block this first route

758
00:54:50,078 --> 00:54:52,080
and make him take the second one.

759
00:54:52,163 --> 00:54:54,124
Once Sato takes this road,

760
00:54:54,207 --> 00:54:56,668
I'll follow him on a bicycle
and shoot him.

761
00:54:57,419 --> 00:54:58,253
No.

762
00:54:58,837 --> 00:54:59,963
This time, I'll do it.

763
00:55:00,046 --> 00:55:03,383
So-ran and the two doctors here.

764
00:55:03,466 --> 00:55:05,010
They may already recognize you

765
00:55:05,093 --> 00:55:07,053
or run into you again someday.

766
00:55:07,137 --> 00:55:08,471
Cheol-yong, my friend.

767
00:55:08,555 --> 00:55:10,098
You're the most at risk.

768
00:55:10,181 --> 00:55:12,392
Your photos are posted
all over Gyeongseong.

769
00:55:12,475 --> 00:55:15,478
Come on. That photo was taken so long ago.

770
00:55:15,562 --> 00:55:17,731
It doesn't look like me anymore, does it?

771
00:55:17,814 --> 00:55:20,525
Don't worry. I'll be fine with a hat on.

772
00:55:20,608 --> 00:55:23,611
Then I'll ride the bicycle.

773
00:55:25,655 --> 00:55:27,115
You've got three kids, right?

774
00:55:27,198 --> 00:55:28,742
Does your family know

775
00:55:28,825 --> 00:55:30,660
you do this outside the clinic?

776
00:55:30,744 --> 00:55:32,037
Not a clue.

777
00:55:32,120 --> 00:55:34,372
But if they find out,
wouldn't they be proud?

778
00:55:34,456 --> 00:55:35,665
I should take the bike--

779
00:55:35,749 --> 00:55:36,750
Forget it.

780
00:55:36,833 --> 00:55:39,044
I was known as Uhm Bok-dong
back in the day.

781
00:55:39,127 --> 00:55:40,086
I'll ride the bike.

782
00:55:40,170 --> 00:55:41,421
If you really want to,

783
00:55:42,130 --> 00:55:45,175
you can block this first route, okay?

784
00:55:45,258 --> 00:55:46,134
Okay.

785
00:55:55,602 --> 00:55:58,313
Detour! Turn around!

786
00:56:05,236 --> 00:56:07,405
What the hell are you doing?

787
00:56:07,489 --> 00:56:08,573
Oh my!

788
00:56:08,656 --> 00:56:09,616
Get it out of here!

789
00:56:09,699 --> 00:56:10,784
What a waste.

790
00:56:10,867 --> 00:56:13,078
- What the hell is this?
- Yes, excuse me.

791
00:56:13,161 --> 00:56:15,372
What are they saying? I'm sorry.

792
00:56:15,455 --> 00:56:17,040
Clean it up already!

793
00:56:17,123 --> 00:56:19,709
Do you think
I understand a word you're jabbering?

794
00:56:19,793 --> 00:56:21,503
It's your fault for blocking the path.

795
00:56:22,003 --> 00:56:23,004
Okay, excuse me.

796
00:56:37,060 --> 00:56:38,311
What floor are you going, sir?

797
00:56:47,779 --> 00:56:49,406
Why are you still here?

798
00:56:49,489 --> 00:56:50,657
You have nothing to do.

799
00:56:51,449 --> 00:56:53,326
You never told me to go home.

800
00:56:54,869 --> 00:56:55,995
You should go home.

801
00:56:56,621 --> 00:56:57,956
Okay, thank you.

802
00:56:59,249 --> 00:57:01,418
Bring me a photo of yourself by tomorrow.

803
00:57:01,501 --> 00:57:03,336
It's for your ID
at the Government-General.

804
00:57:04,963 --> 00:57:06,714
My photo?

805
00:57:07,549 --> 00:57:08,425
Yes.

806
00:57:09,175 --> 00:57:10,635
Is there a problem?

807
00:57:11,261 --> 00:57:12,220
No.

808
00:57:12,303 --> 00:57:13,221
It's just…

809
00:57:13,304 --> 00:57:14,806
I don't have any photos.

810
00:57:18,101 --> 00:57:19,352
Then you can ask him

811
00:57:19,436 --> 00:57:21,980
to take your picture
with the newspaper's camera.

812
00:57:24,149 --> 00:57:25,108
That will work.

813
00:57:33,992 --> 00:57:35,994
- See you tomorrow.
- Have a good night.

814
00:57:42,167 --> 00:57:45,920
Sato is supposed to give a speech
at 7:00 p.m. Monday.

815
00:57:46,004 --> 00:57:48,548
Did you hear anything else
about that schedule?

816
00:57:48,631 --> 00:57:52,427
The briefing for the inspector general
is at 7:00 p.m. the day after tomorrow.

817
00:57:59,350 --> 00:58:01,102
Publications Division, please.

818
00:58:03,855 --> 00:58:07,066
Hello, I'm Keum Seo-hee
from the Foreign Affairs Division.

819
00:58:07,567 --> 00:58:10,945
Mr. Sato Hideo
lent you the English dictionary.

820
00:58:11,029 --> 00:58:13,823
Could I come by
around 7:00 p.m. to pick it up?

821
00:58:18,077 --> 00:58:18,912
All right.

822
00:58:27,295 --> 00:58:29,923
Hello? Yoo Philip from WWP News.

823
00:58:30,590 --> 00:58:31,424
<i>It's me.</i>

824
00:58:31,508 --> 00:58:33,343
Where are you calling from?

825
00:58:34,135 --> 00:58:34,969
<i>What?</i>

826
00:58:35,053 --> 00:58:36,971
I'm at the Government-General.

827
00:58:40,225 --> 00:58:42,143
Are you crazy? Calling me from there?

828
00:58:42,227 --> 00:58:44,395
I called your house, but no one answered.

829
00:58:45,230 --> 00:58:47,232
Why on earth did you call me?

830
00:58:49,400 --> 00:58:50,235
It's just…

831
00:58:51,069 --> 00:58:52,362
Never mind.

832
00:58:53,196 --> 00:58:54,155
Hang on.

833
00:59:16,094 --> 00:59:17,720
No questions. Did you forget?

834
00:59:20,765 --> 00:59:21,933
Wait…

835
00:59:25,853 --> 00:59:26,854
Unbelievable.

836
00:59:26,938 --> 00:59:28,648
I went out of my way to call him.

837
00:59:30,024 --> 00:59:31,901
I spoke to him in English

838
00:59:31,985 --> 00:59:34,737
so the operator wouldn't understand,
but he didn't even thank me.

839
00:59:58,803 --> 01:00:01,014
I see you smelled something sweet.

840
01:00:01,097 --> 01:00:02,557
- Here.
- Thank you.

841
01:00:03,641 --> 01:00:05,184
You want some too?

842
01:00:05,268 --> 01:00:07,186
I can't sell these anyway. Take it.

843
01:00:07,270 --> 01:00:08,229
Thank you.

844
01:00:08,313 --> 01:00:09,606
You should share it!

845
01:00:09,689 --> 01:00:11,649
- Give me some!
- No.

846
01:00:11,733 --> 01:00:12,567
You too?

847
01:00:13,359 --> 01:00:15,194
He'll be here soon.

848
01:00:15,278 --> 01:00:17,655
Get this cleaned up. Hurry!

849
01:00:18,615 --> 01:00:19,616
Here.

850
01:00:19,699 --> 01:00:21,659
- Thank you.
- Take it.

851
01:00:22,201 --> 01:00:23,786
I can't sell them anyway. Here.

852
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:25,580
- Thank you.
- You should share it.

853
01:00:29,083 --> 01:00:32,086
<i>I'm not good at shooting from a distance.</i>

854
01:00:32,170 --> 01:00:34,464
<i>I'll get up against the car window</i>
<i>and shoot.</i>

855
01:00:34,547 --> 01:00:36,633
So-ran, you're great at both.

856
01:00:36,716 --> 01:00:38,843
So please let me have this one, okay?

857
01:00:40,219 --> 01:00:43,598
Come on, let me look cool
for once, please?

858
01:00:43,681 --> 01:00:46,225
We're taking him down
for sure this time! Okay?

859
01:00:46,309 --> 01:00:47,894
Let me get married

860
01:00:47,977 --> 01:00:49,228
before I turn 60.

861
01:01:27,600 --> 01:01:28,851
Got you.

862
01:02:38,838 --> 01:02:41,591
- Where are they shooting from?
- Where did it come from?

863
01:03:52,078 --> 01:03:53,037
Are you all right?

864
01:03:58,918 --> 01:04:00,002
Get in!

865
01:04:02,755 --> 01:04:03,589
Let's go.

866
01:04:06,551 --> 01:04:08,553
Damn it. Get in!

867
01:04:17,812 --> 01:04:18,813
Get in!

868
01:04:34,120 --> 01:04:35,955
Jeong-yeop, stay awake.

869
01:04:36,038 --> 01:04:37,707
You have to stay awake.

870
01:04:38,583 --> 01:04:39,750
Wake up, you punk!

871
01:04:41,294 --> 01:04:43,296
Jeong-yeop!

872
01:04:51,470 --> 01:04:52,305
Chief!

873
01:05:11,198 --> 01:05:12,575
Over there!

874
01:05:13,326 --> 01:05:14,243
Over there!

875
01:06:32,780 --> 01:06:36,701
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

876
01:06:37,618 --> 01:06:38,786
<i>Madam</i>.

877
01:06:38,869 --> 01:06:40,579
<i>After the shootout last night,</i>

878
01:06:41,914 --> 01:06:44,125
we're hunting down
the remaining fugitives.

879
01:06:45,126 --> 01:06:47,294
<i>- Out of the office?</i>
<i>- Orders from above.</i>

880
01:06:47,378 --> 01:06:50,798
I was going to call
for a Joseon language interpreter.

881
01:06:50,881 --> 01:06:52,174
<i>I'd appreciate your help.</i>

882
01:06:52,258 --> 01:06:53,926
<i>Why are you using that name?</i>

883
01:06:54,010 --> 01:06:54,969
<i>Unless you want to…</i>

884
01:06:55,052 --> 01:06:56,345
<i>Please let me go.</i>

885
01:06:56,429 --> 01:06:58,889
…watch your family die in front of you.

886
01:07:00,891 --> 01:07:02,059
<i>I suppose…</i>

887
01:07:02,143 --> 01:07:03,436
You people are insane!

888
01:07:04,145 --> 01:07:05,771
<i>…this is where it ends.</i>

889
01:07:14,780 --> 01:07:16,782
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
